artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a chemical reaction network (CRN) consisting of a finite set of chemical species $\{X_i\}_{i=1}^N$ and a finite set of elementary reversible reactions $\{\rho\}_{\rho=1}^R$ described by a stoichiometric matrix $S \in \mathbb{Z}^{N \times R}$, where $S_{i\rho}$ is the net change in the abundance of species $X_i$ due to one occurrence of reaction $\rho$. The deterministic dynamics of concentrations $z_i(t)$ obey $\dot z = S\, j(z)$, where $j_\rho(z) = j_\rho^+(z) - j_\rho^-(z)$ are mass-action currents satisfying local detailed balance: $\ln\bigl(j_\rho^+(z)/j_\rho^-(z)\bigr) = -\Delta_\rho\mu(z)/(k_BT)$, where $\mu_i(z) = \mu_i^\circ + k_BT \ln z_i$ are chemical potentials and $\Delta_\rho\mu = \sum_i S_{i\rho}\mu_i$. A subset $Y \subseteq \{1,\dots,N\}$ of species is chemostatted, with concentrations held fixed by external reservoirs.

For such a (generally nonlinear) open CRN, develop a systematic framework of nonequilibrium thermodynamics that:

(a) identifies a complete and minimal set of independent thermodynamic forces — linear combinations of the chemical potentials of the chemostatted species, determined by the cokernel structure of $S$ restricted to $Y$ — that drive the steady-state molecular currents and account for the entropy production rate $\dot\Sigma \ge 0$;

(b) constructs an explicit nonequilibrium free-energy (semistate) functional $\mathcal{F}(z)$, depending on the network topology and on the choice of chemostatted species, that quantifies the free energy stored inside the CRN, satisfies a second-law inequality $\dot{\mathcal{F}} \le \dot W_{\text{driv}} - T\dot\Sigma$ for an appropriately defined driving work $\dot W_{\text{driv}}$, and reduces to the standard relative entropy when the system is detailed-balanced;

(c) defines, for arbitrary partitions of the reactions into "input" and "output" subnetworks, a thermodynamically consistent notion of energy transduction efficiency $\eta$ between coupled chemical processes operating far from equilibrium, and characterizes the topological conditions on $(S,Y)$ under which $\eta$ is bounded away from $0$ and approaches its theoretical maximum;

(d) applies this framework to large metabolic networks to relate quantitative performance measures (dissipation, stored free energy, transduction efficiency, robustness of stationary fluxes) to graph-theoretic and stoichiometric invariants of $(S,Y)$, such as the dimensions of $\ker S$, $\mathrm{coker}\, S$, and the cycle/cocycle decomposition of the reaction hypergraph.

Provide such a formulation, or determine the maximal class of nonlinear CRNs for which (a)–(d) admit a fully consistent and computable solution.

